Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs) are tradable instruments that represent the environmental attributes of electricity generated from eligible renewable sources, including solar, wind, biomass, geothermal and hydropower. Each certificate typically represents one megawatt-hour (MWh) of renewable electricity generation. RECs allow organizations to document renewable electricity procurement separately from the physical electricity they consume.

The market is gaining momentum as utilities, technology companies, manufacturers and other corporate buyers seek credible ways to meet renewable energy targets. Regulatory requirements, sustainability reporting expectations and digital certificate tracking systems are contributing to market development. Companies are increasingly evaluating REC procurement as part of broader strategies to manage electricity-related emissions and demonstrate progress toward environmental commitments.

